{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0275-when-should-one-stop-the-most.md", "equation": "Binary posterior / win probability:** Π_s = P(θ=1 | 𝒢_s), a bounded martingale. The Bayes decision risk E[∫₀^ν f(s)ds + ℓ(θ,d)] is reduced (§2) to the optimal stopping problem **inf_{ν≤S} E[∫₀^ν f(s)ds + g(Π_ν)]**, where g(π) = inf_d [πℓ(1,d) + (1−π)ℓ(0,d)] is the concave terminal cost induced by the optimal terminal action.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9123}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0275-when-should-one-stop-the-most.md", "equation": "Separable win-martingale + time change:** assume dΠ_s = ρ(s)σ(Π_s)dW_s. Define A(s) = ∫₀^s ρ²(v)dv with inverse Γ, and X_t = Π_{Γ(t)}; then dX_t = σ(X_t)dB_t. The deterministic time factor is absorbed into the transformed running cost **c(t) = f(Γ(t))Γ′(t) = f(Γ(t))/ρ²(Γ(t))**. Transformed value: **V_T(t,x) = inf_{τ≤T−t} E[∫₀^τ c(t+u)du + g(X^x_τ)]** with generator **ℒ = ½σ²(x)∂_xx**.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9123}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0275-when-should-one-stop-the-most.md", "equation": "Lagrange formulation:** W(t,x) = V(t,x) − g(x) solves **W(t,x) = inf_τ E[∫₀^τ (c(t+u) + ℒg(X^x_u))du]** — the object on which monotonicity and smooth-fit arguments are run.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9123}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0275-when-should-one-stop-the-most.md", "equation": "Lamperti transform** used to linearize space: L^l via Ψ(x) = ∫ 1/σ, turning the diffusion into unit-volatility with drift μ.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9123}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0275-when-should-one-stop-the-most.md", "equation": "Bayes reduction (§2):** inf_{ν≤S} E[∫₀^ν f(s)ds + g(Π_ν)], g(π) = inf_d[πℓ(1,d) + (1−π)ℓ(0,d)].", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9123}
